  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how aquatic food webs change along several environmental gradients. From left to right, aquatic species richness increases, and the community shifts from a simple system with one trophic level to more complex systems with two and then three trophic levels. The vertical axis indicates increasing temperature upward.\n\nAt the base of all versions are microbes, above them phytoplankton, then zooplankton, and finally fish and shrimp in the most complex web. Wavy arrows between levels suggest feeding links or energy transfer through the food web. In the simplest case, only lower trophic groups are present, and higher consumers are absent, marked by red X symbols. With greater richness, more kinds of microbes, phytoplankton, and zooplankton appear, and top consumers become established.\n\nThe bottom arrows show that these biological changes are associated with broader environmental trends: nutrients increase toward the right, salinity decreases toward the right, and CO₂ release also increases toward the right. Overall, the image conveys that warming and changing aquatic conditions are linked to shifts in biodiversity and trophic structure, from simplified communities to richer, more layered food webs.",
